::ENG2_U05_PRE_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which sentence uses standard English correctly?{
~A. Students needs a quiet place after school.#Incorrect. Students needs should be students need.
=B. Students need a quiet place to complete assignments after school.#Correct. The sentence is complete and uses standard English conventions.
~C. Because students need a quiet place.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
~D. Students need quiet, the hallways are loud.#Incorrect. This is a comma splice.
}



::ENG2_U05_PRE_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.V.02] Which pair shows a word and a derivation?{
~A. quiet and loud#Incorrect. These are opposites, not derivations.
~B. school and hallway#Incorrect. These are related by topic.
~C. good and nice#Incorrect. These are similar in meaning, not a derivation pair.
=D. revise and revision#Correct. Revision is derived from revise.
}



::ENG2_U05_PRE_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which word has the most formal connotation for a school proposal?{
=A. beneficial#Correct. Beneficial is formal and positive.
~B. cool#Incorrect. Cool is informal.
~C. awesome#Incorrect. Awesome is casual.
~D. neat#Incorrect. Neat is informal.
}



::ENG2_U05_PRE_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which revision best fits school leaders as the audience?{
~A. The library should stay open because it is cool.#Incorrect. This is informal and vague.
~B. Kids need it because it is good.#Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
=C. Extended library hours would provide students with a supervised place to complete assignments after school.#Correct. This revision is formal, specific, and audience-aware.
~D. I like the library a lot.#Incorrect. This is personal and not audience-focused.
}



::ENG2_U05_PRE_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.V.01] Which phrase uses academic vocabulary most appropriately?{
=A. supervised academic workspace#Correct. This phrase is precise and appropriate for school writing.
~B. cool place#Incorrect. This is informal.
~C. good thing#Incorrect. This is vague.
~D. nice stuff#Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
}



::ENG2_U05_PRE_Q06::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] What is the clearest meaning of this sentence? "Students who wait for late buses need a supervised study space."{
~A. Students should leave school before buses arrive.#Incorrect. The sentence says students wait for late buses.
~B. Teachers should ride late buses.#Incorrect. Teachers are not the focus.
~C. The library should close early.#Incorrect. The sentence supports the need for a study space.
=D. Some students need a monitored place to study while they wait after school.#Correct. This accurately restates the sentence.
}



::ENG2_U05_PRE_Q07::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which sentence fixes the error in "students cannot focus good"?{
~A. students cannot focus good#Incorrect. The error remains.
=B. students cannot focus well#Correct. Well correctly describes how students focus.
~C. students cannot focused good#Incorrect. This creates another error.
~D. students cannot good focus#Incorrect. The word order is not standard.
}



::ENG2_U05_PRE_Q08::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] What is denotation?{
~A. The feeling a word creates#Incorrect. That is connotation.
~B. The punctuation after a word#Incorrect. That is a convention issue.
=C. The dictionary meaning of a word#Correct. Denotation is a word's literal or dictionary meaning.
~D. The origin of a word#Incorrect. That is etymology.
}



::ENG2_U05_PRE_Q09::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which feedback is most actionable?{
=A. Replace vague wording with specific details about which students need support.#Correct. This feedback gives the writer a clear revision action.
~B. Make it better.#Incorrect. This is too vague.
~C. I do not like it.#Incorrect. This does not identify a revision step.
~D. Change everything.#Incorrect. This is too broad to guide revision.
}



::ENG2_U05_PRE_Q10::[MLA.ENG2.V.02] Which word belongs in the same word family as precise?{
~A. prepare#Incorrect. Prepare is unrelated.
~B. pressure#Incorrect. Pressure is unrelated in meaning.
=C. precision#Correct. Precision is the noun form related to precise.
~D. present#Incorrect. Present is not part of the precise word family.
}
